UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 12, Lesson 2: Writing a novel response', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"An extended novel response plans sections (craft/character/theme), keeps summary brief and evaluates how successfully the novel achieves an effect for you as reader, using evidence.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nCreate a four-box plan with evidence bullets for an extended response question set by your teacher (or: How does the novel explore trust?).\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Boxes analytical, not retell; evidence listed.\n\nCHECK\nRetelling the whole plot. Control summary ruthlessly.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a 300–400 word novel response from your plan with minimal summary and clear evaluation.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"An extended novel response plans sections (craft/character/theme), keeps summary brief and evaluates how successfully the novel achieves an effect for you as reader, using evidence.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Writing","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN4-ECA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a 300–400 word novel response from your plan with minimal summary and clear evaluation. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using extended response on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Retelling the whole plot.. Correction: Strong answer: Boxes analytical, not retell;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Retelling the whole plot. Control summary ruthlessly. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating extended response on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with extended response, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about extended response with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for extended response. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for extended response. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retelling the whole plot","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Boxes analytical, not retell"],"question":"Create a four-box plan with evidence bullets for an extended response question set by your teacher (or: How does the novel explore trust?). Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for extended response; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An extended novel response plans sections (craft/character/theme), keeps summary brief and evaluates how successfully","Boxes analytical, not retell","Careful use of planning grid and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Retelling the whole plot"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with extended response?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named planning grid and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply extended response to new material and name the planning grid and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Retelling the whole plot","Name planning grid and explain why it matters only, without linking it to extended response"],"question":"Which response best applies extended response with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries extended response. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retelling the whole plot","Point to exact words in the sentence that show extended response and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words"],"question":"Read: \"Beside the oval, Kai practised bowling until the sun slipped low.\" Which choice best uses extended response from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name planning grid and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","Boxes analytical, not retell","Retelling the whole plot"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about extended response?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea","Locate precise textual evidence, then explain how language choices shape meaning or effect"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse planning grid and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for extended response?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retelling the whole plot","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about extended response","Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of extended response on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about extended response with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating extended response on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using extended response on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for extended response. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with extended response, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Retelling the whole plot.. Correction: Strong answer: Boxes analytical, not retell;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-eng-y7-w12-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise extended response.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use extended response with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify planning grid and explain why it matters.","I can practise controlled summary independently on a new prompt.","I can notice evaluation needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"An extended novel response plans sections (craft/character/theme), keeps summary brief and evaluates how successfully the novel achieves an effect for you as reader, using evidence.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation","An extended novel response plans sections (craft/character/theme), keeps summary brief and evaluates how successfully"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about extended response with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPlan: para1 character relationships; para2 motif/theme; para3 craft hooks; close evaluation. Summary capped at two sentences total across the piece.\n\nTRY FIRST\nCreate a four-box plan with evidence bullets for an extended response question set by your teacher (or: How does the novel explore trust?).\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Boxes analytical, not retell; evidence listed.","explain":"An extended novel response plans sections (craft/character/theme), keeps summary brief and evaluates how successfully the novel achieves an effect for you as reader, using evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Retelling the whole plot. Control summary ruthlessly. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Extended response.","caption":"Analyse more than retell.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Plan</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">section boxes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Summary</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">tight cap</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Plan: para1 character relationships; para2 motif/theme; para3 craft hooks; close evaluation. Summary capped at two sente Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Plan: para1 character relationships; para2 motif/theme; para3 craft hooks; close evaluation. Summary capped at two sente Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about extended response with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPlan: para1 character relationships; para2 motif/theme; para3 craft hooks; close evaluation. Summary capped at two sentences total across the piece. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Reader evaluation.","caption":"Judge with proof.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Evaluate</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">how successful</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Evidence</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">throughout</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about extended response with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRetelling the whole plot. Control summary ruthlessly. Here is a more productive model: Plan: para1 character relationships; para2 motif/theme; para3 craft hooks; close evaluation. Summary capped at two sentences total across the piece.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Boxes analytical, not retell; evidence listed.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Retelling the whole plot. Control summary ruthlessly. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about extended response with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAn extended novel response plans sections (craft/character/theme), keeps summary brief and evaluates how successfully the novel achieves an effect for you as reader, using evidence. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 7, Week 12, Lesson 2: Writing a novel response","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Novel study","week":12,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nPlan: para1 character relationships; para2 motif/theme; para3 craft hooks; close evaluation. Summary capped at two sentences total across the piece.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Extended response.","caption":"Analyse more than retell.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Plan</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">section boxes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Summary</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">tight cap</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Reader evaluation.","caption":"Judge with proof.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><